<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Diomedes</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Diomedes was a Greek hero of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trojan-war">Trojan War</a>, king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and one of the bravest fighters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>. Later legend told that on his return from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> he sailed to southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> and founded Arpi and other towns in Daunia, northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>. The Daunians and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> accepted the story, and he was worshipped as a hero in several places around the Adriatic.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/diomedes.jpg" alt="Diomedes" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Copy of Nausykles or Kresilas (?) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Diomedes_Louvre_Ma890_n3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>In myth Diomedes was the son of Tydeus and Deipyle. Tydeus, an Aetolian of royal blood and the son of Oeneus, king of Calydon, was exiled for killing relatives and found refuge at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a>, where King Adrastus gave him his daughter Deipyle in marriage. Tydeus died among the Seven against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes">Thebes</a>, and according to the Iliad Diomedes was then a small child. He later succeeded his maternal grandfather Adrastus as king of Argos.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/homer">Homer</a>, Diomedes came to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> with 80 ships, a contribution third only to those of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agamemnon">Agamemnon</a> (100) and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a> (90), with Sthenelus, his charioteer and probably closest friend, and Euryalus fighting under him. He is the key fighter in the first third of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iliad">Iliad</a>. He is the only human besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heracles">Heracles</a> granted strength to fight immortals, and he wounded both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ares">Ares</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphrodite">Aphrodite</a> in a single day, yet he retreated before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a>, which some interpreters read as his keeping within mortal limits.</p><p>Diomedes was a companion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odysseus">Odysseus</a>, and both were favourites of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a>. After the seer Helenus revealed that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/troy">Troy</a> would not fall while the Palladium, an image of Athena, stood inside its walls, the two stole it, and Diomedes is generally regarded as the one who carried it to the ships. After Troy fell, Diomedes and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nestor">Nestor</a> decided to leave at once, and Diomedes reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> four days later, one of the few Achaean commanders to return safely.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Tradition says Diomedes went to Daunia in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apulia">Apulia</a>, where King Daunus gave him land and his daughter Euippe in return for help against the Messapians. He built Argyrippa, hosted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneas">Aeneas</a> there and gave back the Palladium, as an oracle had told him to give it to a Trojan. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> credited him with founding many towns in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a>, among them Arpi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benevento">Beneventum</a>, Brundusium, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canusium">Canusium</a>, Venafrum, Ancona, Adria and Venusia.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> lists four traditions of his end: he went home to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> and died, he stayed at Urium, he disappeared on the island of Diomedea in the Adriatic, whose birds were said to be his companions, or he stayed among the Heneti and was mysteriously deified. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pindar">Pindar</a> says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> made him immortal. Hero cults of Diomedes are attested in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, Metapontum and along the Adriatic coast. At Argos his shield was carried through the streets with the Palladium at the festival of Athena. In the Inferno Dante places him in the Eighth Circle of Hell, among the counsellors of fraud.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Diomedes” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A two-word stock epithet can fill half a line, freeing the poet to compose the rest, and small inconsistencies, such as Aphrodite remaining “laughter-loving” after <b>Diomedes</b> has painfully wounded her, may be effects of that tradition.</blockquote><a href="/work/iliad">Iliad · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Greek legend made the hero <b>Diomedes</b>, returning from Troy, the founder of Arpi and other towns, a story the Daunians themselves seem to have adopted.</blockquote><a href="/state/daunians">Daunians</a></li></ul></section>
